course: dictionary
lesson: 10292
title: Totally: completely, or just really
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Totally means completely, in every way. The sentence around it shows you how much. «Have you ever dreamed you could fly and it felt totally real, until you woke up?» Totally real means completely real, not a little bit real.
explain: In chatty talk, children say «totally!» to mean «yes, I agree completely». It makes the yes much stronger, a bit like saying «really».
explain: To check your meaning, swap in «completely». «Totally different» means completely different, and «totally fine» means completely fine. If the sentence still makes sense, you have got it.
fact: Totally means completely, in every way.
fact: «Totally» is an adverb, so it tells you more about another word, such as «real» in «totally real».
fact: In casual speech, «totally» can mean «yes, I agree strongly» or make something stronger, like «really».
fact: Phrases you may meet: «totally different», «totally agree», «totally forgot», «totally fine».
fact: Regional slang: «pure mental» (Glasgow) means totally mad; «wicked» (Maine) means very.
exercise: «Have you ever dreamed you could fly and it felt totally real, until you woke up?» What does totally mean here? | Not at all | Quickly | A little bit | *Completely
exercise: Ella says, «I totally agree!» What does totally mean? | Sadly | No, I don't agree | Very slowly | *Yes, I agree strongly
exercise: Which phrase means completely forgot? | *totally forgot | totally sleepy | totally cold | totally quiet
test: «The fairground ride was totally scary.» Which word could replace totally? | *Completely | Very quietly | Slowly
test: Ben says, «Yeah, totally!» What is he doing? | Asking a question | *Agreeing strongly | Saying no | Feeling sleepy
exam: «The shop was totally empty.» Which meaning fits best? | Empty of people but full of toys | Only a little bit empty | *Completely empty | Half empty
